@attribute [Route(pageUrl)]
@attribute [Route(pageUrl + "/{Sample}")]
@layout DemosMainLayout

<SamplePageHeader ChartKey="@SampleChartKeys.LineChart" Sample="@Sample">
    <PageMetaTags PageUrl="@pageUrl" Title="@metaTitle" Description="@metaDescription" ImageUrl="@imageUrl" />

    <PageHero Title="@pageTitle">
        <SubTitleTemplate>
            @((MarkupString)pageDescription)
        </SubTitleTemplate>
    </PageHero>

    <DocsLink Href="@DemoRouteConstants.Docs_LineChart" />

    <Prerequisites PageUrl="@pageUrl" />
</SamplePageHeader>

@* Each SampleSection renders only on its own sample page, /demos/<chart>/<slug>. *@
<CascadingValue Value="@SampleCatalog.Find(SampleChartKeys.LineChart, Sample)">
<SampleSection Slug="how-it-works">
    <Block>
        The <strong>Line Chart</strong> component visualizes data trends using connected data points, making it ideal for tracking changes over time or comparing multiple series.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>How to use:</strong>
        <div class="content">
            <ol>
                <li>Define your chart data by specifying <code>Labels</code> for the X-axis and one or more <code>Datasets</code> for the Y-axis values.</li>
                <li>Customize the chart appearance and behavior using the <code>Options</code> property (e.g., colors, line styles, tooltips, axis settings).</li>
                <li>Add the <code>LineChart</code> component to your page and bind your data and options.</li>
                <li>Refer to the demo code below for examples of basic usage, advanced customization, and dynamic updates.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>
        These demos demonstrate how to set up a line chart, configure its appearance, and update the data or options to fit your application's needs.
    </Block>
    <Demo Type="typeof(LineChart_Demo_01_A_Examples)" Tabs="true" />
    <Block>
        <p>
            You can further enhance your charts by updating datasets, changing styles, or handling chart events. Review the demo code for practical scenarios and copy the relevant parts into your own project to get started quickly.
        </p>
    </Block>
    <Demo Type="typeof(LineChart_Demo_01_B_Examples)" Tabs="true" />
</SampleSection>

<SampleSection Slug="playground">
    <Block>
        Change the main options of a line chart and watch the chart and its C# update together. The controls cover tension, line width, point radius, stepped lines and fill, plus the legend, the locale and right-to-left layout.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>How to use:</strong>
        <div class="content">
            <ol>
                <li>Change a control. The chart is updated with <code>UpdateAsync</code>, so it animates from its current state.</li>
                <li>Pick a <strong>Locale</strong> to see how numbers are formatted for that region, and switch on <strong>Right to left</strong> to mirror the legend and tooltip.</li>
                <li>Copy the C# below the chart. It lists only the settings that differ from their defaults, with the property names the library uses.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>
    </Block>
    <LineChart_Playground />
</SampleSection>

<SampleSection Slug="combo-bar-line">
    <Block>
        The <strong>Combo bar/line</strong> demo mixes <code>LineChartDataset</code> and <code>BarChartDataset</code> in the same <code>LineChart</code>.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>How to use:</strong>
        <div class="content">
            <ol>
                <li>Use the <code>LineChart</code> component as the root chart.</li>
                <li>Add both <code>LineChartDataset</code> and <code>BarChartDataset</code> instances to the same <code>ChartData.Datasets</code> collection.</li>
                <li>Configure interaction options such as <code>Mode = InteractionMode.Index</code> and <code>Intersect = false</code> so bar and line points share tooltips cleanly.</li>
                <li>Refer to the demo code below for a working example with a line series and bar columns in the same chart area.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>
    </Block>
    <Demo Type="typeof(LineChart_Demo_06_Combo_Bar_Line)" Tabs="true" />
</SampleSection>

<SampleSection Slug="stacked-line-chart">
    <Block>
        The <strong>Stacked line chart</strong> demo shows how to combine <code>LineChartDataset.Stack</code> with a stacked Y-axis to build a layered line or area chart.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>How to use:</strong>
        <div class="content">
            <ol>
                <li>Set <code>Options.Scales.Y.Stacked = true</code> so values accumulate on the Y-axis.</li>
                <li>Assign the same <code>Stack</code> value to each <code>LineChartDataset</code> that should contribute to the same stacked total.</li>
                <li>Optionally set <code>Fill</code> or use <code>FillToStackedValueBelow()</code> to make each layer easier to read.</li>
                <li>Refer to the demo code below for a working example of stacked traffic series using shared stack IDs.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>
    </Block>
    <Demo Type="typeof(LineChart_Demo_07_Stack)" Tabs="true" />
</SampleSection>

<SampleSection Slug="data-labels">
    <Block>
        The <strong>Line Chart</strong> component supports data labels, allowing you to display values directly on each data point in the chart.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>How to use:</strong>
        <div class="content">
            <ol>
                <li>Enable data labels by passing the <code>ChartDataLabels</code> plugin when calling <code>InitializeAsync</code>: <code>plugins: new[] { "ChartDataLabels" }</code>.</li>
                <li>Customize the label content, formatting, and position using <code>Options.Plugins.Datalabels</code> or per-dataset <code>Datalabels</code> settings.</li>
                <li>Bind your data and labels to the chart as usual.</li>
                <li>Refer to the demo code below for a working example and further configuration options.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>
        Data labels make it easier for users to interpret the chart by showing the exact value of each point directly on the line.
    </Block>
    <Demo Type="typeof(LineChart_Demo_02_Datalabels)" Tabs="true" />
</SampleSection>

<SampleSection Slug="tick-configuration">
    <Block>
        The <strong>Tick configuration</strong> demo shows how to customize the appearance and behavior of axis ticks in the Line Chart component.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>How to use:</strong>
        <div class="content">
            <ol>
                <li>Use the <code>Options.Scales.X.Ticks</code> and <code>Options.Scales.Y.Ticks</code> properties to control tick display, such as step size, min/max values, and formatting.</li>
                <li>You can format tick labels, hide or show specific ticks, and adjust their appearance to improve chart readability.</li>
                <li>Apply custom logic for tick callbacks to display values in a specific format (e.g., currency, percentage).</li>
                <li>Refer to the demo code below for practical examples of configuring axis ticks for your data visualization needs.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>
        Customizing tick configuration helps ensure your chart is clear and tailored to your application's requirements.
    </Block>
    <Demo Type="typeof(LineChart_Demo_03_Tick_Configuration)" Tabs="true" />
</SampleSection>

<SampleSection Slug="locale">
    <Block>
        The <strong>Locale</strong> demo illustrates how to localize number and date formatting in the Line Chart component to match your users' regional preferences.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>How to use:</strong>
        <div class="content">
            <ol>
                <li>Set the <code>Locale</code> property on the <code>LineChart</code> component to your desired culture code (e.g., <code>"de-DE"</code> for German).</li>
                <li>All number and date labels in the chart will automatically format according to the specified locale, ensuring correct separators, symbols, and date formats.</li>
                <li>This is especially useful for applications targeting users in different regions, providing a familiar and accessible data presentation.</li>
                <li>Refer to the demo code below for a working example and additional configuration options.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>
        Adapting the chart's locale improves usability and ensures your data is presented in a way that aligns with your audience's expectations.
    </Block>
    <Demo Type="typeof(LineChart_Demo_04_Locale)" Tabs="true" />
</SampleSection>

<SampleSection Slug="interpolation-modes">
    <Block>
        The <strong>Interpolation Modes</strong> demo showcases how different interpolation settings affect the appearance and smoothness of lines in the <code>LineChart</code> component. Interpolation determines how data points are connected, allowing you to choose between straight lines, smooth curves, or monotone cubic curves for more natural transitions.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>How to use:</strong>
        <div class="content">
            <ol>
                <li>Prepare your chart data by defining <code>Labels</code> for the X-axis and one or more <code>Datasets</code> for the Y-axis values.</li>
                <li>For each dataset, set the <code>CubicInterpolationMode</code> property to control the interpolation style (e.g., <code>"monotone"</code> for monotone cubic interpolation, or leave unset for linear).</li>
                <li>Optionally, adjust the <code>Tension</code> property to fine-tune the curve smoothness.</li>
                <li>Add the <code>LineChart</code> component to your page, binding your data and options as shown in the demo code below.</li>
                <li>Experiment with different interpolation modes and tension values to achieve the desired visual effect for your data.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>
        Using interpolation modes helps you present trends more clearly, making your charts easier to interpret and visually appealing. Refer to the demo code for practical implementation details.
    </Block>
    <Demo Type="typeof(LineChart_Demo_05_Interpolation_Modes)" Tabs="true" />
</SampleSection>

<SampleSection Slug="click-event">
    <Block>
        Set the <code>OnClick</code> callback to react when a line data point is selected. The callback receives <code>ChartClickEventArgs</code> with the dataset, point index, label, and raw value; the demo displays the selected item below the chart.
    </Block>
    <Demo Type="typeof(LineChart_Demo_08_Click_Event)" Tabs="true" />
</SampleSection>

<SampleSection Slug="export-image">
    <Block>
        Export the chart as an image with <code>ToBase64ImageAsync</code>, which returns a base64 data URL you can show, store or send, or save it straight to a file in the browser with <code>DownloadImageAsync</code>.
        If the chart is still animating, both wait for the animation to finish, and the image is streamed from the browser, so Blazor Server needs no larger message size.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>How to use:</strong>
        <div class="content">
            <ol>
                <li>Keep a reference to the chart with <code>@@ref</code> and initialize it.</li>
                <li>Call <code>ToBase64ImageAsync()</code> for a PNG data URL, or pass <code>ChartImageType.Jpeg</code> and a quality from 0 to 1.</li>
                <li>Call <code>DownloadImageAsync("line-chart.png")</code> to save the image as a file without sending it to .NET.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>
    </Block>
    <Demo Type="typeof(LineChart_Demo_09_Export_Image)" Tabs="true" />
</SampleSection>

<SampleSection Slug="show-and-hide-datasets">
    <Block>
        Show and hide whole datasets or single points from C#, check whether a dataset is visible and count the visible datasets.
        Visibility changed at runtime, from C# or a legend click, stays in place when <code>UpdateAsync</code> refreshes the data, because datasets are matched by <code>Key</code>, or else by label, and points by label.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>How to use:</strong>
        <div class="content">
            <ol>
                <li>Call <code>HideAsync(datasetIndex)</code> and <code>ShowAsync(datasetIndex)</code>, or <code>SetDatasetVisibilityAsync(datasetIndex, visible)</code>.</li>
                <li>Pass a data index too, for example <code>HideAsync(0, 2)</code>, to hide one point. This hides the point's marker only; the line still passes through its value.</li>
                <li>Read the state with <code>IsDatasetVisibleAsync</code> and <code>GetVisibleDatasetCountAsync</code>.</li>
                <li>Refresh with <code>UpdateAsync(data, options, ChartUpdateMode.None)</code> to apply new data without animation; hidden datasets and points stay hidden.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>
    </Block>
    <Demo Type="typeof(LineChart_Demo_10_Show_Hide_Datasets)" Tabs="true" />
</SampleSection>

<SampleSection Slug="legend-click-event">
    <Block>
        Set <code>OnLegendClick</code> to react when a legend item is clicked. The callback receives <code>ChartLegendClickEventArgs</code> with the dataset index, the legend text and whether the dataset is hidden after the click.
        Set <code>LegendClickToggles="false"</code> to stop the legend from hiding datasets, for example to open details instead.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>How to use:</strong>
        <div class="content">
            <ol>
                <li>Add an <code>OnLegendClick</code> callback to the chart.</li>
                <li>Keep <code>LegendClickToggles</code> at <code>true</code> for the default hide and show, or set it to <code>false</code> to handle the click yourself.</li>
                <li>Use <code>DatasetIndex</code>, <code>Text</code> and <code>Hidden</code> from the event arguments.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>
    </Block>
    <Demo Type="typeof(LineChart_Demo_11_Legend_Click_Event)" Tabs="true" />
</SampleSection>

<SampleSection Slug="hover-event">
    <Block>
        Set <code>OnHover</code> to react when the pointer moves onto a different point. The callback receives <code>ChartHoverEventArgs</code> with the same item details as <code>OnClick</code>.
        It is called only when the hovered point changes, and once with a <code>null</code> <code>DatasetIndex</code> when the pointer leaves all points, so it does not flood Blazor Server with calls.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>How to use:</strong>
        <div class="content">
            <ol>
                <li>Add an <code>OnHover</code> callback to the chart.</li>
                <li>Check <code>DatasetIndex</code>: a value means a point is hovered; <code>null</code> means the pointer left all points.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>
    </Block>
    <Demo Type="typeof(LineChart_Demo_12_Hover_Event)" Tabs="true" />
</SampleSection>

<SampleSection Slug="programmatic-highlight">
    <Block>
        Highlight points and show their tooltip from C# with <code>SetActiveElementsAsync</code>, for example to point at a value from a table next to the chart.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>How to use:</strong>
        <div class="content">
            <ol>
                <li>Pass one or more <code>ChartActiveElement(datasetIndex, index)</code> items to <code>SetActiveElementsAsync</code>.</li>
                <li>Pass an empty list to clear the highlight and the tooltip.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>
    </Block>
    <Demo Type="typeof(LineChart_Demo_13_Programmatic_Highlight)" Tabs="true" />
</SampleSection>

<SampleSection Slug="update-modes-and-animation-control">
    <Block>
        Choose how <code>UpdateAsync</code> animates with <code>ChartUpdateMode</code>, and control the running animation with <code>StopAsync</code>, <code>RenderAsync</code>, <code>ResetAsync</code> and <code>ClearAsync</code>.
        These map one-to-one to the Chart.js <code>update(mode)</code>, <code>stop()</code>, <code>render()</code>, <code>reset()</code> and <code>clear()</code> methods.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>How to use:</strong>
        <div class="content">
            <ol>
                <li>Call <code>UpdateAsync(data, options)</code> for the default animated update, or pass <code>ChartUpdateMode.None</code> to apply the change at once. An animated update moves each matched dataset from its current values to the new ones, and a new dataset grows in. Datasets are matched by <code>Key</code> when set, otherwise by <code>Label</code>; datasets that share a label, or have none, pair in order.</li>
                <li>Call <code>StopAsync()</code> to end any running animation; the chart stays as it was drawn at that moment. Call <code>UpdateAsync</code> again to animate to new values.</li>
                <li>Call <code>ResetAsync()</code> to put the chart back in its starting state, before its first animation, and <code>RenderAsync()</code> to draw that state. Stop any running animation first, or its next frame draws over the reset. Call <code>ResetAsync()</code> and then <code>UpdateAsync(data, options)</code> to regrow the chart from its starting state, as on first render.</li>
                <li>Call <code>ClearAsync()</code> to clear the canvas; <code>RenderAsync()</code> draws the chart again. A running animation draws over the cleared canvas on its next frame, so stop it first or wait for it to end.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>
    </Block>
    <Demo Type="typeof(LineChart_Demo_14_Update_Modes)" Tabs="true" />
</SampleSection>

<SampleSection Slug="multiple-axes">
    <Block>
        Add more axes with <code>Scales.AdditionalAxes</code>, keyed by axis ID, and point a dataset at one with <code>YAxisID</code>. Chart.js works out the direction from the first letter of the ID, so <code>"y1"</code> is a y-axis, and orders axes on the same edge by <code>Weight</code>.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>How to use:</strong>
        <div class="content">
            <ol>
                <li>Set <code>Scales.AdditionalAxes = new() { ["y1"] = new ChartAxes { Position = "right" } }</code>.</li>
                <li>Set <code>Grid = new ChartAxesGrid { DrawOnChartArea = false }</code> on the extra axis so only one set of grid lines crosses the chart.</li>
                <li>Set <code>YAxisID = "y1"</code> on the dataset that uses the extra axis; the others stay on <code>y</code>.</li>
                <li>To put both axes on the same edge, set <code>Position = "left"</code> on both; the axis with the higher <code>Weight</code> is further from the chart.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>
    </Block>
    <Demo Type="typeof(LineChart_Demo_15_Multiple_Axes)" Tabs="true" />
</SampleSection>

<SampleSection Slug="axis-position-and-reverse">
    <Block>
        Move an axis to another edge with <code>Position</code>, flip its direction with <code>Reverse</code>, or hide it with <code>Display = false</code>. A hidden axis still scales the data; only its labels, ticks and grid lines go.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>How to use:</strong>
        <div class="content">
            <ol>
                <li>Set <code>Scales.X.Position = "top"</code> or <code>Scales.Y.Position = "right"</code>.</li>
                <li>Set <code>Scales.Y.Reverse = true</code> to put the lowest value at the top, for example for a ranking where 1 is best.</li>
                <li>Set <code>Scales.X.Reverse = true</code> to list the labels from right to left.</li>
                <li>Set <code>Scales.Y.Display = false</code> to hide an axis.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>
    </Block>
    <Demo Type="typeof(LineChart_Demo_16_Axis_Position_And_Reverse)" Tabs="true" />
</SampleSection>

<SampleSection Slug="tick-options">
    <Block>
        <code>ChartAxesTicks</code> controls how many ticks an axis gets and how their labels look. Chart.js picks the tick spacing itself unless you set <code>StepSize</code> or <code>Count</code>, and skips crowded labels unless you set <code>AutoSkip = false</code>.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>How to use:</strong>
        <div class="content">
            <ol>
                <li>Set <code>StepSize</code> for the gap between ticks, <code>Precision</code> for the number of decimals, or <code>Count</code> for an exact number of ticks on a value axis; <code>MaxTicksLimit</code> caps the number of ticks.</li>
                <li>Set <code>AutoSkip = false</code> with <code>MinRotation</code> and <code>MaxRotation</code> (degrees) to show every label, rotated to fit.</li>
                <li>Set <code>Font = new ChartFont { Size = 14, Weight = "bold" }</code> and <code>Color</code> to style the labels.</li>
                <li>Set <code>Mirror = true</code> to draw the y labels inside the chart, <code>CrossAlign = "far"</code> to align them away from the axis, and <code>LabelOffset</code> to move labels along the axis, in pixels.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>
    </Block>
    <Demo Type="typeof(LineChart_Demo_17_Tick_Options)" Tabs="true" />
</SampleSection>

<SampleSection Slug="axis-title">
    <Block>
        Label an axis with <code>ChartAxesTitle</code>. The title is hidden until <code>Display = true</code>, and in this library it defaults to <code>"black"</code> text in a bold 12 px font, where Chart.js uses grey.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>How to use:</strong>
        <div class="content">
            <ol>
                <li>Set <code>Scales.Y.Title = new ChartAxesTitle { Display = true, Text = "Visitors (k)" }</code>.</li>
                <li>Set <code>Color</code> and <code>Font = new ChartFont { Size = 14, Style = "italic" }</code> to style it.</li>
                <li>Set <code>Padding = 10</code> for the same space above and below, or <code>new ChartPadding { Top = 12, Bottom = 0 }</code>.</li>
                <li>Set <code>TitleAlignment = TitleAlignment.Start</code> or <code>TitleAlignment.End</code> to move it along the axis.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>
    </Block>
    <Demo Type="typeof(LineChart_Demo_18_Axis_Title)" Tabs="true" />
</SampleSection>

<SampleSection Slug="grace-and-bounds">
    <Block>
        <code>Grace</code> adds space beyond the data, <code>Bounds</code> decides whether the axis ends at the data (<code>"data"</code>) or at a whole tick (<code>"ticks"</code>, the Chart.js default), and <code>SuggestedMin</code> / <code>SuggestedMax</code> extend the range only when the data does not already reach them. Value axes in this library start at zero (<code>BeginAtZero = true</code>), so the demos set <code>BeginAtZero = false</code> to show both ends.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>How to use:</strong>
        <div class="content">
            <ol>
                <li>Set <code>Scales.Y.Grace = "10%"</code> for a share of half the data range (Chart.js measures it from the middle to each end), or <code>Grace = 5</code> for data units.</li>
                <li>Set <code>Scales.Y.Bounds = "data"</code> to end the axis at the lowest and highest values instead of the nearest ticks.</li>
                <li>Set <code>SuggestedMax = 100</code> or <code>SuggestedMin</code> for a range the data can still go past; <code>Min</code> and <code>Max</code> fix it.</li>
                <li>To stack two axes on one edge, give both the same <code>Stack</code> and <code>Position</code>, size them with <code>StackWeight</code>, and set <code>Offset = true</code> for space between them.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>
    </Block>
    <Demo Type="typeof(LineChart_Demo_19_Grace_And_Bounds)" Tabs="true" />
</SampleSection>

<SampleSection Slug="span-gaps">
    <Block>
        A <code>null</code> value leaves a gap, and by default Chart.js breaks the line there. <code>SpanGaps = true</code> joins the points on either side, and <code>MaxSpanGap</code> breaks the line only where two neighbouring points are further apart on the index axis than that number.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>How to use:</strong>
        <div class="content">
            <ol>
                <li>Put <code>null</code> in <code>Data</code> where a value is missing, for example <code>[12, 15, null, 18]</code>.</li>
                <li>Set <code>SpanGaps = true</code> on the <code>LineChartDataset</code> to draw the line across the gaps.</li>
                <li>Set <code>MaxSpanGap = 1</code> to join points at most 1 unit apart; when set, it is sent as <code>spanGaps</code> instead of <code>SpanGaps</code>.</li>
                <li><code>MaxSpanGap</code> is measured in index-axis units, and on a category axis every label is 1 unit apart, so use it on a linear or time x-axis (<code>Scales.X.Type = ChartAxesType.Linear</code>) with missing points.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>
    </Block>
    <Demo Type="typeof(LineChart_Demo_20_Span_Gaps)" Tabs="true" />
</SampleSection>

<SampleSection Slug="clip">
    <Block>
        When an axis has a fixed <code>Min</code> or <code>Max</code>, Chart.js cuts the line at the edge of the chart area. <code>ClipSides</code> moves that edge per side, in pixels: a positive value lets the line overflow, a negative value cuts it inside the chart area.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>How to use:</strong>
        <div class="content">
            <ol>
                <li>Set <code>Scales.Y.Min = 0</code> and <code>Scales.Y.Max = 100</code> with data that goes past them.</li>
                <li>Set <code>ClipSides = new ChartClip { Top = 20, Bottom = 20, Left = 0, Right = 0 }</code> on the dataset to let it overflow by 20 px at the top and bottom.</li>
                <li>Set negative values, such as <code>-10</code> on every side, to cut the line inside the chart area.</li>
                <li>A side you leave unset is sent as <code>0</code> (clip at the chart area), because Chart.js needs all four. When set, <code>ClipSides</code> is sent instead of <code>Clip</code>.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>
    </Block>
    <Demo Type="typeof(LineChart_Demo_21_Clip)" Tabs="true" />
</SampleSection>

<SampleSection Slug="filler-options">
    <Block>
        <code>Plugins.Filler</code> sets how the area under filled lines is drawn. With <code>Propagate = true</code> (the Chart.js default), a dataset whose fill target is hidden fills to that target's own target instead; <code>DrawTime</code> sets when the fills are drawn.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>How to use:</strong>
        <div class="content">
            <ol>
                <li>Set <code>Fill = "origin"</code> on the first dataset and <code>Fill = "-1"</code> on the next ones to fill to the dataset before.</li>
                <li>Set <code>Hidden = true</code> on a middle dataset, or click it in the legend.</li>
                <li>Set <code>Plugins.Filler = new ChartPluginsFiller { Propagate = true }</code> to fill past the hidden dataset, or <code>Propagate = false</code> to leave that fill out.</li>
                <li>Set <code>DrawTime = "beforeDraw"</code> to draw the fills under the grid lines, <code>"beforeDatasetsDraw"</code> to draw them before any line, or leave the default <code>"beforeDatasetDraw"</code>.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>
    </Block>
    <Demo Type="typeof(LineChart_Demo_22_Filler_Options)" Tabs="true" />
</SampleSection>

<SampleSection Slug="hover-line">
    <Block>
        Line charts draw a vertical line through the point the tooltip shows. It is a plugin of this library, not of Chart.js, and draws a grey 2 px dashed line unless you set <code>Plugins.TooltipLine</code>.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>How to use:</strong>
        <div class="content">
            <ol>
                <li>Set <code>Interaction = new Interaction { Mode = InteractionMode.Index, Intersect = false }</code> so the tooltip, and the line, follow the pointer along the x-axis.</li>
                <li>Leave <code>Plugins.TooltipLine</code> unset for the default line.</li>
                <li>Set <code>Plugins.TooltipLine = new() { Color = "red", Width = 1, Dash = [] }</code> for a solid red 1 px line; an empty <code>Dash</code> draws a solid line.</li>
                <li>Set <code>Plugins.TooltipLine = new() { Display = false }</code> to hide it.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>
    </Block>
    <Demo Type="typeof(LineChart_Demo_23_Hover_Line)" Tabs="true" />
</SampleSection>

<SampleSection Slug="title-and-subtitle">
    <Block>
        <code>Plugins.Title</code> and <code>Plugins.Subtitle</code> draw text outside the chart area; Chart.js places the subtitle between the title and the chart. Both are hidden until <code>Display = true</code>, and in this library their text defaults to <code>"black"</code>.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>How to use:</strong>
        <div class="content">
            <ol>
                <li>Set <code>Plugins.Title = new ChartPluginsTitle { Display = true, Text = "Website visitors" }</code>.</li>
                <li>Set <code>Plugins.Subtitle = new ChartPluginsSubtitle { Display = true, Text = "January to June" }</code>.</li>
                <li>Set <code>Position</code> to <code>"top"</code>, <code>"left"</code>, <code>"bottom"</code> or <code>"right"</code>, and <code>Align</code> to <code>"start"</code>, <code>"center"</code> or <code>"end"</code>.</li>
                <li>Set <code>Padding = new ChartPadding { Top = 10, Bottom = 20 }</code> for space around the title, and <code>FullSize = false</code> to size it to the chart area instead of the canvas.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>
    </Block>
    <Demo Type="typeof(LineChart_Demo_24_Title_And_Subtitle)" Tabs="true" />
</SampleSection>

<SampleSection Slug="tooltip-options">
    <Block>
        <code>Plugins.Tooltip</code> chooses which items the tooltip lists and how it looks. Its <code>Mode</code> and <code>Intersect</code> override <code>Interaction</code> for the tooltip only; Chart.js uses <code>Nearest</code> with <code>Intersect = true</code> by default.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>How to use:</strong>
        <div class="content">
            <ol>
                <li>Set <code>Plugins.Tooltip = new ChartPluginsTooltip { Mode = InteractionMode.Index, Intersect = false }</code> to list every dataset at the hovered label.</li>
                <li>Set <code>Position = "average"</code> (the Chart.js default) or <code>"nearest"</code> to place the tooltip.</li>
                <li>Set <code>Padding</code>, <code>CornerRadius</code>, <code>BorderColor</code> and <code>BorderWidth</code> to style the box, and <code>MultiKeyBackground</code> for the fill behind each color box.</li>
                <li>Set <code>BoxWidth</code>, <code>BoxHeight</code> and <code>BoxPadding</code> to size the color boxes, or <code>UsePointStyle = true</code> to draw each dataset's <code>PointStyle</code> instead.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>
    </Block>
    <Demo Type="typeof(LineChart_Demo_25_Tooltip_Options)" Tabs="true" />
</SampleSection>

<SampleSection Slug="layout-padding">
    <Block>
        <code>Layout</code> adds empty space inside the canvas, around the legend, axes and chart. The dashed borders show the canvas edges.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>How to use:</strong>
        <div class="content">
            <ol>
                <li>Set <code>Layout.Padding = 20</code> for the same padding on every side, in pixels.</li>
                <li>Set <code>Layout.PaddingSides = new() { Left = 60, Top = 10 }</code> for a value per side; when set, it is sent instead of <code>Padding</code>.</li>
                <li>Leave both unset for no padding.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>
    </Block>
    <Demo Type="typeof(LineChart_Demo_26_Layout_Padding)" Tabs="true" />
</SampleSection>

<SampleSection Slug="aspect-ratio-and-resize-delay">
    <Block>
        With <code>MaintainAspectRatio = true</code>, the canvas height follows its width by <code>AspectRatio</code> (width / height); Chart.js uses 2 for line charts. <code>ResizeDelay</code> waits the given milliseconds after the container stops resizing before the chart redraws, which saves work while the window is dragged; resize the window to see the wide chart catch up.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>How to use:</strong>
        <div class="content">
            <ol>
                <li>Set <code>MaintainAspectRatio = true</code> (the default) and <code>AspectRatio = 1</code>, <code>2</code> or <code>3</code>.</li>
                <li>Set <code>ResizeDelay = 300</code> to redraw once, 300 ms after a resize ends; Chart.js uses 0.</li>
                <li>Set <code>DevicePixelRatio = 2</code> to draw twice as many canvas pixels, for sharper exported images.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>
    </Block>
    <Demo Type="typeof(LineChart_Demo_27_Aspect_Ratio_And_Resize_Delay)" Tabs="true" />
</SampleSection>

<SampleSection Slug="hover-and-elements">
    <Block>
        <code>Elements</code> sets defaults for every element of a kind, such as every point or line, and a dataset value wins over them. <code>Hover</code> sets which elements react to the pointer, separately from <code>Interaction</code>, and <code>Events</code> lists the browser events Chart.js listens to.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>How to use:</strong>
        <div class="content">
            <ol>
                <li>Set <code>Elements = new ChartElements { Point = new ChartElementsPoint { Radius = 5, PointStyle = "rectRot", HoverRadius = 9 } }</code> and leave the point settings unset on the datasets.</li>
                <li>Set <code>Elements.Line = new ChartElementsLine { BorderDash = [6, 4] }</code> for dashed lines. A <code>LineChartDataset</code> always sends its own <code>Tension</code> and <code>BorderWidth</code>, so set those on the dataset.</li>
                <li>Set <code>Hover = new ChartHover { Mode = InteractionMode.Index, Intersect = false }</code> to enlarge every point of the hovered label; any value left unset falls back to <code>Interaction</code>.</li>
                <li>Set <code>Events = ["click"]</code> to react to clicks only: moving the pointer does nothing and <code>OnHover</code> does not fire; clicking a point still highlights it and opens its tooltip, and <code>OnClick</code> fires.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>
    </Block>
    <Demo Type="typeof(LineChart_Demo_28_Hover_And_Elements)" Tabs="true" />
</SampleSection>

<SampleSection Slug="colors-plugin">
    <Block>
        The Chart.js colors plugin gives each dataset a color from its built-in palette, but only when no dataset sets a color. Line, radar and scatter datasets always send a default color (<code>"rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.1)"</code>), so on a line chart the palette needs <code>ForceOverride = true</code>.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>How to use:</strong>
        <div class="content">
            <ol>
                <li>Set <code>Plugins.Colors = new ChartPluginsColors { ForceOverride = true }</code> to color the datasets from the palette.</li>
                <li><code>ForceOverride = true</code> also replaces <code>BackgroundColor</code> and <code>BorderColor</code> set on the datasets.</li>
                <li>Without <code>ForceOverride</code>, <code>Enabled = true</code> (the Chart.js default) has no effect on line datasets; set <code>Enabled = false</code> to turn the plugin off.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>
    </Block>
    <Demo Type="typeof(LineChart_Demo_29_Colors_Plugin)" Tabs="true" />
</SampleSection>

<SampleSection Slug="time-axis">
    <Block>
        A <strong>time axis</strong> spaces points by the real time between them: two days apart takes twice the room of one. It needs no extra script; the library draws it with its own date adapter.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>How to use:</strong>
        <div class="content">
            <ol>
                <li>Set <code>ChartData.TimeLabels</code> to dates (<code>DateOnly</code>, <code>DateTime</code> or <code>DateTimeOffset</code>) and give each dataset one value per date in <code>Data</code>.</li>
                <li>Set <code>Scales.X = new ChartAxes { Type = ChartAxesType.Time }</code>.</li>
                <li>Pick the tick unit with <code>Time.Unit</code>, and the label and tooltip formats with .NET date format strings, for example <code>DisplayFormats.Day = "dd MMM"</code> and <code>TooltipFormat = "dddd, dd MMMM yyyy"</code>.</li>
                <li>A <code>DateOnly</code>, or a <code>DateTime</code> whose <code>Kind</code> is <code>Unspecified</code>, shows the same date and time in every time zone.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>
    </Block>
    <Demo Type="typeof(LineChart_Demo_30_Time_Axis)" Tabs="true" />
</SampleSection>

<SampleSection Slug="time-series">
    <Block>
        A <strong>time series axis</strong> keeps date labels but gives every data point the same width, which suits trading days or data with long gaps.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>How to use:</strong>
        <div class="content">
            <ol>
                <li>Use the same <code>TimeLabels</code> and <code>Data</code> as a time axis.</li>
                <li>Set <code>Type = ChartAxesType.Timeseries</code> on the x-axis.</li>
                <li>Set <code>Ticks.Source = "labels"</code> to put one tick on each date.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>
    </Block>
    <Demo Type="typeof(LineChart_Demo_31_Time_Series)" Tabs="true" />
</SampleSection>

<SampleSection Slug="time-combo-bar-line">
    <Block>
        Bars and a line can share a time axis: both datasets take one value per date in <code>TimeLabels</code>.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>How to use:</strong>
        <div class="content">
            <ol>
                <li>Add a <code>LineChartDataset</code> and a <code>BarChartDataset</code> to the same <code>ChartData</code>, with <code>TimeLabels</code> set to the first day of each month.</li>
                <li>Set <code>Time.Unit = ChartTimeUnit.Month</code> and <code>DisplayFormats.Month = "MMM yyyy"</code>.</li>
                <li>Set <code>Offset = true</code> on the x-axis so the first and last bars are not cut in half.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>
    </Block>
    <Demo Type="typeof(LineChart_Demo_32_Time_Combo_Bar_Line)" Tabs="true" />
</SampleSection>

<SampleSection Slug="time-sensor-readings">
    <Block>
        When each series has its own times, such as sensors reporting at different moments, give every value its own date with <code>TimeData</code> instead of shared labels.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>How to use:</strong>
        <div class="content">
            <ol>
                <li>Set <code>TimeData</code> on each <code>LineChartDataset</code> to a list of <code>new ChartTimePoint(date, value)</code>. Leave <code>ChartData.TimeLabels</code> and <code>Data</code> unset.</li>
                <li>A <code>null</code> value leaves a gap in the line.</li>
                <li>Use <code>Unit = ChartTimeUnit.Minute</code> and <code>DisplayFormats.Minute = "HH:mm"</code> for readings within an hour.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>
    </Block>
    <Demo Type="typeof(LineChart_Demo_33_Time_Sensor_Readings)" Tabs="true" />
</SampleSection>

<SampleSection Slug="time-live-streaming">
    <Block>
        Time charts can grow while they are shown: add a reading at its own time, or a new day with a value for each dataset.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>How to use:</strong>
        <div class="content">
            <ol>
                <li>For a reading, call <code>AddDataAsync(chartData, "Sensor", new LineChartDatasetData("Sensor", time, value))</code>. The label argument is the dataset's label, and the point is appended to its <code>TimeData</code>.</li>
                <li>For a new day, call <code>AddDataAsync(chartData, new DateOnly(2026, 1, 6), new LineChartDatasetData("Daily sales", 24))</code>. The date is added to <code>ChartData.TimeLabels</code>.</li>
                <li>Click the buttons to add data to each chart.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>
    </Block>
    <Demo Type="typeof(LineChart_Demo_34_Time_Live_Streaming)" Tabs="true" />
</SampleSection>

<SampleSection Slug="time-max-span">
    <Block>
        Fix the visible part of a time axis to a range of dates, for example the last 14 days of a quarter's data.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>How to use:</strong>
        <div class="content">
            <ol>
                <li>Set <code>MinTime</code> and <code>MaxTime</code> on the x-axis to dates. They are sent instead of the numeric <code>Min</code> and <code>Max</code>.</li>
                <li>Use <code>SuggestedMinTime</code> and <code>SuggestedMaxTime</code> instead to widen the range without cutting off data.</li>
                <li>Change the range and call <code>UpdateAsync</code>; the buttons switch between 14 days, 30 days and all data.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>
    </Block>
    <Demo Type="typeof(LineChart_Demo_35_Time_Max_Span)" Tabs="true" />
</SampleSection>

<SampleSection Slug="time-localized-formats">
    <Block>
        Month and weekday names in date formats follow <code>ChartOptions.Locale</code>, or the browser's language when it is not set.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>How to use:</strong>
        <div class="content">
            <ol>
                <li>Set <code>Locale</code>, for example <code>"fr-FR"</code>; <code>"ddd d MMM"</code> then shows <em>lun. 5 janv.</em></li>
                <li>Set <code>Unit = ChartTimeUnit.Week</code> and <code>IsoWeekday = DayOfWeek.Monday</code> to start each week on Monday.</li>
                <li>Click a locale to redraw the chart with its names.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>
    </Block>
    <Demo Type="typeof(LineChart_Demo_36_Time_Localized_Formats)" Tabs="true" />
</SampleSection>

<SampleSection Slug="tick-and-tooltip-functions">
    <Block>
        Some Chart.js options take a JavaScript function, for example a tick label or a tooltip line. Register the function in your own JavaScript file, then reference it from C# by name with <code>ChartFunction</code>. See <a href="@DemoRouteConstants.Docs_Getting_Started_Introduction#javascript-functions">JavaScript functions</a> for setup.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>How to use:</strong>
        <div class="content">
            <ol>
                <li>
                    Register the functions after <code>blazorexpress.chartjs.js</code> has loaded:
                    <pre><code>blazorexpress.chartjs.registerFunction('demo.thousandsLabel', value => `${value / 1000}k units`);
blazorexpress.chartjs.registerFunction('demo.unitsTooltipLabel',
    item => `${item.dataset.label}: ${item.parsed.y.toLocaleString()} units`);</code></pre>
                </li>
                <li>Set <code>Scales.Y.Ticks = new ChartAxesTicks { Callback = new ChartFunction("demo.thousandsLabel") }</code>.</li>
                <li>Set <code>Plugins.Tooltip = new ChartPluginsTooltip { Callbacks = new ChartPluginsTooltipCallbacks { Label = new ChartFunction("demo.unitsTooltipLabel") } }</code>.</li>
                <li>Hover a point: the tooltip shows the text the function returns.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>
    </Block>
    <Demo Type="typeof(LineChart_Demo_37_Tick_And_Tooltip_Functions)" Tabs="true" />
</SampleSection>

<SampleSection Slug="legend-filter-function">
    <Block>
        A legend <code>Filter</code> function decides which legend items are shown. The datasets it hides are still drawn.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>How to use:</strong>
        <div class="content">
            <ol>
                <li>
                    Register the filter, which returns <code>true</code> to keep an item:
                    <pre><code>blazorexpress.chartjs.registerFunction('demo.hideTargetLegendItems', legendItem => !legendItem.text.startsWith('Target'));</code></pre>
                </li>
                <li>Set <code>Plugins.Legend.Labels = new ChartPluginsLegendLabels { Filter = new ChartFunction("demo.hideTargetLegendItems") }</code>.</li>
                <li><code>Sort</code> and <code>GenerateLabels</code> work the same way.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>
    </Block>
    <Demo Type="typeof(LineChart_Demo_38_Legend_Filter_Function)" Tabs="true" />
</SampleSection>

<SampleSection Slug="segment-styling">
    <Block>
        <code>SegmentStyle</code> styles each part of the line between two points. Here, segments that span a missing reading are drawn dashed and grey, as in the Chart.js "Line Segment Styling" sample.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>How to use:</strong>
        <div class="content">
            <ol>
                <li>
                    Register functions that get the segment context (<code>p0</code>, <code>p1</code>) and return a value, or <code>undefined</code> to keep the dataset's style:
                    <pre><code>const touchesGap = ctx => ctx.p0.skip || ctx.p1.skip;
blazorexpress.chartjs.registerFunction('demo.dashOverGaps', ctx => touchesGap(ctx) ? [6, 6] : undefined);
blazorexpress.chartjs.registerFunction('demo.greyOverGaps', ctx => touchesGap(ctx) ? 'rgb(160, 160, 160)' : undefined);</code></pre>
                </li>
                <li>Use <code>null</code> for missing values and set <code>SpanGaps = true</code>.</li>
                <li>Set <code>SegmentStyle = new ChartLineSegment { BorderDashFunction = new ChartFunction("demo.dashOverGaps"), BorderColorFunction = new ChartFunction("demo.greyOverGaps") }</code>. Plain values such as <code>BorderDash = [4, 4]</code> style every segment.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>
    </Block>
    <Demo Type="typeof(LineChart_Demo_39_Segment_Styling)" Tabs="true" />
</SampleSection>

<SampleSection Slug="gradient-fill">
    <Block>
        A colour function can return a canvas gradient. Chart.js calls it while drawing, so the gradient follows the chart area when the chart resizes.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>How to use:</strong>
        <div class="content">
            <ol>
                <li>
                    Register a function that builds the gradient once the chart area is known:
                    <pre><code>blazorexpress.chartjs.registerFunction('demo.verticalGradient', context => {
    const { ctx, chartArea } = context.chart;
    if (!chartArea) return 'rgba(54, 162, 235, 0.2)';
    const gradient = ctx.createLinearGradient(0, chartArea.bottom, 0, chartArea.top);
    gradient.addColorStop(0, 'rgba(54, 162, 235, 0)');
    gradient.addColorStop(1, 'rgba(54, 162, 235, 0.6)');
    return gradient;
});</code></pre>
                </li>
                <li>Set <code>Fill = true</code> and <code>BackgroundColorFunction = new ChartFunction("demo.verticalGradient")</code>. When set, it is sent instead of <code>BackgroundColor</code>.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>
    </Block>
    <Demo Type="typeof(LineChart_Demo_40_Gradient_Fill)" Tabs="true" />
</SampleSection>

<SampleSection Slug="stepped-line">
    <Block>
        <code>Stepped</code> draws a line as horizontal and vertical steps instead of a slope between points. It suits values that change at a moment and then hold, such as a stock level, a price or an on/off state.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>How to use:</strong>
        <div class="content">
            <ol>
                <li>Set <code>Stepped = true</code> on a <code>LineChartDataset</code>: the line holds each value until the next point, then steps to the new value.</li>
                <li>Or choose where the step is drawn: <code>"before"</code> is the same as <code>true</code>, <code>"after"</code> steps straight after each point and then holds the new value, and <code>"middle"</code> steps halfway between two points. <code>false</code>, the default, draws straight lines.</li>
                <li>Call <code>UpdateAsync</code> after changing the value on a chart that is already drawn.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>
    </Block>
    <Demo Type="typeof(LineChart_Demo_41_Stepped_Line)" Tabs="true" />
</SampleSection>

<SampleSection Slug="point-styling">
    <Block>
        <code>PointStyle</code> sets the shape drawn at each data point, and <code>PointRadius</code> its size. A different shape per dataset tells lines apart without relying on colour.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>How to use:</strong>
        <div class="content">
            <ol>
                <li>Set <code>PointStyle</code> on a <code>LineChartDataset</code> to one of <code>circle</code>, <code>cross</code>, <code>crossRot</code>, <code>dash</code>, <code>line</code>, <code>rect</code>, <code>rectRounded</code>, <code>rectRot</code>, <code>star</code> or <code>triangle</code>.</li>
                <li>Give one value to style every point, or one value per point to style them individually. <code>PointRadius</code>, <code>PointHoverRadius</code> and <code>PointBorderWidth</code> work the same way.</li>
                <li>Set <code>Plugins.Legend.Labels.UsePointStyle = true</code> to draw the same shape in the legend.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>
    </Block>
    <Demo Type="typeof(LineChart_Demo_42_Point_Styling)" Tabs="true" />
</SampleSection>

<SampleSection Slug="line-styling">
    <Block>
        <code>BorderWidth</code>, <code>BorderDash</code>, <code>BorderCapStyle</code> and <code>Fill</code> change how a line is drawn. The demo shows a plain line, a dashed line with round caps and a filled, thicker line in one chart.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>How to use:</strong>
        <div class="content">
            <ol>
                <li>Set <code>BorderColor</code> and <code>BorderWidth</code> for the line itself.</li>
                <li>Set <code>BorderDash</code> to a list of lengths, in pixels: drawn, skipped, drawn, and so on. <code>[5, 5]</code> gives even dashes.</li>
                <li>Set <code>BorderCapStyle = "round"</code> to round both ends of every dash. A round cap adds half the line width to each end, so leave a wider gap between the dashes.</li>
                <li>Set <code>Fill = true</code> to fill the area under the line with <code>BackgroundColor</code>.</li>
                <li>Set <code>BorderJoinStyle = "round"</code> for rounded corners where two segments of the line meet.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>
    </Block>
    <Demo Type="typeof(LineChart_Demo_43_Line_Styling)" Tabs="true" />
</SampleSection>

<SampleSection Slug="area-fill-boundaries">
    <Block>
        <code>Fill</code> sets where the filled area of a line ends: the zero line, the bottom of the chart area or its top. The data in the demo crosses zero, so each boundary looks different.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>How to use:</strong>
        <div class="content">
            <ol>
                <li>Set <code>Fill = "origin"</code> to fill between the line and zero.</li>
                <li>Set <code>Fill = "start"</code> to fill down to the bottom of the chart area, or <code>"end"</code> to fill up to its top.</li>
                <li>Set <code>Fill = false</code>, the default, for no fill. The helpers <code>FillToOrigin()</code>, <code>FillToStart()</code> and <code>FillToEnd()</code> set the same values.</li>
                <li>Use a translucent <code>BackgroundColor</code> so the grid stays visible through the fill.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>
    </Block>
    <Demo Type="typeof(LineChart_Demo_44_Area_Fill_Boundaries)" Tabs="true" />
</SampleSection>

<SampleSection Slug="fill-between-datasets">
    <Block>
        A line can fill to another dataset instead of to the axis, which draws a band between two lines. The demo fills a range of low, average and high values.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>How to use:</strong>
        <div class="content">
            <ol>
                <li>Call <code>FillToDataset(index)</code> on a <code>LineChartDataset</code> to fill to the dataset at that index in the chart.</li>
                <li>Or pass <code>relativeIndex: true</code> to count from the dataset itself: <code>FillToDataset(-1, relativeIndex: true)</code> fills to the dataset before it.</li>
                <li>Leave <code>Plugins.Filler.Propagate</code> at its default, <code>true</code>, so a band is still drawn when its target dataset is hidden from the legend.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>
    </Block>
    <Demo Type="typeof(LineChart_Demo_45_Fill_Between_Datasets)" Tabs="true" />
</SampleSection>

<SampleSection Slug="linear-scale-min-max">
    <Block>
        <code>Min</code> and <code>Max</code> fix the range of a linear axis, and cut off values beyond it. <code>SuggestedMin</code> and <code>SuggestedMax</code> only widen the range, so the axis still grows to show every value.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>How to use:</strong>
        <div class="content">
            <ol>
                <li>Set <code>Options.Scales.Y.Min</code> and <code>Max</code> for a range that never changes, for example a percentage from 0 to 100.</li>
                <li>Set <code>SuggestedMin</code> and <code>SuggestedMax</code> for a range that the data may exceed.</li>
                <li>Leave all four unset to let Chart.js fit the axis to the data. The library sets <code>BeginAtZero = true</code> by default, so the fitted axis includes zero; set <code>BeginAtZero = false</code> to fit the data only.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>
    </Block>
    <Demo Type="typeof(LineChart_Demo_46_Linear_Scale_Min_Max)" Tabs="true" />
</SampleSection>

<SampleSection Slug="logarithmic-scale">
    <Block>
        A logarithmic axis gives each power of ten the same height, so small and very large values fit in one chart. Switch the demo to a linear axis to see the small values flatten out.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>How to use:</strong>
        <div class="content">
            <ol>
                <li>Set <code>Options.Scales.Y.Type = "logarithmic"</code>.</li>
                <li>Set <code>BeginAtZero = false</code>: a logarithmic axis cannot show zero or negative values.</li>
                <li>Set <code>Type = "linear"</code>, or leave it unset, to go back to a linear axis.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>
    </Block>
    <Demo Type="typeof(LineChart_Demo_47_Logarithmic_Scale)" Tabs="true" />
</SampleSection>

<SampleSection Slug="stacked-scales">
    <Block>
        Axes with the same <code>Stack</code> value share one side of the chart, one above the other, each with its own range. It shows two unrelated measures over the same period without overlapping lines.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>How to use:</strong>
        <div class="content">
            <ol>
                <li>Add the second axis with <code>Options.Scales.AdditionalAxes</code>, and point a dataset at it with <code>YAxisID</code>.</li>
                <li>Set the same <code>Stack</code> value and the same <code>Position</code> on both axes.</li>
                <li>Set <code>StackWeight</code> to share the height: an axis with weight 2 gets twice the height of one with weight 1.</li>
                <li>Set <code>Offset = true</code> on an axis to keep a gap between it and its neighbour.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>
    </Block>
    <Demo Type="typeof(LineChart_Demo_48_Stacked_Scales)" Tabs="true" />
</SampleSection>

<SampleSection Slug="center-axis-position">
    <Block>
        <code>Position = "center"</code> draws an axis through the middle of the chart area instead of along its edge, as in a mathematical plot.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>How to use:</strong>
        <div class="content">
            <ol>
                <li>Set <code>Options.Scales.X.Position = "center"</code> and <code>Options.Scales.Y.Position = "center"</code>.</li>
                <li>Give the y-axis the same range on both sides of zero with <code>Min</code> and <code>Max</code>, so the centred x-axis sits at zero.</li>
                <li>Use an odd number of labels so the centred y-axis sits on the middle label.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>
    </Block>
    <Demo Type="typeof(LineChart_Demo_49_Center_Axis_Position)" Tabs="true" />
</SampleSection>

<SampleSection Slug="grid-configuration">
    <Block>
        <code>Grid</code> styles the grid lines and tick marks of an axis, and <code>Border</code> styles the axis line. Each axis has its own settings.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>How to use:</strong>
        <div class="content">
            <ol>
                <li>Set <code>Options.Scales.X.Grid</code> or <code>Options.Scales.Y.Grid</code> to a <code>ChartAxesGrid</code>.</li>
                <li>Use <code>Color</code> and <code>LineWidth</code> for the grid lines, and <code>TickColor</code> and <code>TickLength</code> for the marks between the axis and its labels.</li>
                <li>Set <code>DrawOnChartArea = false</code> to keep the tick marks and drop the lines across the chart, or <code>Display = false</code> to drop both.</li>
                <li>Set <code>Border</code> to a <code>ChartAxesBorder</code> for the axis line; its <code>Dash</code> makes the grid lines of that axis dashed.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>
    </Block>
    <Demo Type="typeof(LineChart_Demo_50_Grid_Configuration)" Tabs="true" />
</SampleSection>

<SampleSection Slug="legend-point-style">
    <Block>
        <code>UsePointStyle</code> draws each dataset's point shape in the legend instead of a coloured box. With a different shape per dataset, the legend can be read without relying on colour.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>How to use:</strong>
        <div class="content">
            <ol>
                <li>Give each <code>LineChartDataset</code> its own <code>PointStyle</code>.</li>
                <li>Set <code>Options.Plugins.Legend.Labels = new ChartPluginsLegendLabels { UsePointStyle = true }</code>.</li>
                <li>Optionally set <code>PointStyleWidth</code> on the labels to change the width of the shape in the legend.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>
    </Block>
    <Demo Type="typeof(LineChart_Demo_51_Legend_Point_Style)" Tabs="true" />
</SampleSection>

<SampleSection Slug="legend-title">
    <Block>
        <code>Legend.Title</code> adds a heading above the legend items. It says what the datasets have in common, such as "Sales region".
        <br /><br />
        <strong>How to use:</strong>
        <div class="content">
            <ol>
                <li>Set <code>Options.Plugins.Legend.Title</code> to a <code>ChartPluginsLegendTitle</code> with <code>Display = true</code> and a <code>Text</code>.</li>
                <li>Set <code>Color</code> and <code>Padding</code> to style it.</li>
                <li>The title follows the legend: move both with <code>Options.Plugins.Legend.Position</code>.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>
    </Block>
    <Demo Type="typeof(LineChart_Demo_52_Legend_Title)" Tabs="true" />
</SampleSection>

<SampleSection Slug="legend-position">
    <Block>
        <code>Legend.Position</code> places the legend on any side of the chart, and <code>Legend.Align</code> moves it along that side.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>How to use:</strong>
        <div class="content">
            <ol>
                <li>Set <code>Options.Plugins.Legend.Position</code> to <code>"top"</code>, <code>"left"</code>, <code>"bottom"</code> or <code>"right"</code>. The default is <code>"top"</code>.</li>
                <li>Set <code>Options.Plugins.Legend.Align</code> to <code>"start"</code>, <code>"center"</code> or <code>"end"</code>. The default is <code>"center"</code>.</li>
                <li>Call <code>UpdateAsync</code> to move the legend on a chart that is already drawn.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>
    </Block>
    <Demo Type="typeof(LineChart_Demo_53_Legend_Position)" Tabs="true" />
</SampleSection>

<SampleSection Slug="interaction-modes">
    <Block>
        <code>Interaction.Mode</code> decides which points the pointer selects, for both the hover highlight and the tooltip. <code>Intersect</code> decides whether the pointer has to be on a point.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>How to use:</strong>
        <div class="content">
            <ol>
                <li>Set <code>Options.Interaction = new Interaction { Mode = InteractionMode.Index, Intersect = false }</code> to select every dataset's point at the pointer's x position.</li>
                <li>Use <code>Nearest</code> for the single closest point, <code>Point</code> for the points under the pointer, <code>Dataset</code> for the whole line, and <code>X</code> or <code>Y</code> for points that share a coordinate with the pointer.</li>
                <li>Set <code>Intersect = true</code> to react only when the pointer is on a point.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>
    </Block>
    <Demo Type="typeof(LineChart_Demo_54_Interaction_Modes)" Tabs="true" />
</SampleSection>

<SampleSection Slug="html-tooltip">
    <Block>
        <code>Tooltip.External</code> replaces the canvas tooltip with an HTML element that a registered JavaScript function builds. Use it when a tooltip needs your own fonts, layout or styles.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>How to use:</strong>
        <div class="content">
            <ol>
                <li>Register the function shown below in your own script file. It builds the tooltip with <code>createElement</code> and <code>textContent</code>, so labels are always shown as text.</li>
                <li>Set <code>Options.Plugins.Tooltip = new ChartPluginsTooltip { Enabled = false, External = new ChartFunction("demo.htmlTooltip") }</code>. <code>Enabled = false</code> turns the canvas tooltip off.</li>
                <li>Set <code>Options.Interaction</code> as usual: it still decides which points the tooltip shows.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>
    </Block>
    <CodeChunk Class="mb-3" LanguageCode="LanguageCode.JavaScript" FilePath="~\Pages\Demos\LineChart\LineChart_Demo_55_Html_Tooltip_Snippet.js" ProjectRootClassType="DemoAppConstants.ProjectRootClassType" />
    <Demo Type="typeof(LineChart_Demo_55_Html_Tooltip)" Tabs="true" />
</SampleSection>

<SampleSection Slug="animation-loop">
    <Block>
        <code>Animation.Loop</code> repeats the chart animation forwards and backwards without end. Use it sparingly: constant motion draws the eye, and some people find it uncomfortable.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>How to use:</strong>
        <div class="content">
            <ol>
                <li>Set <code>Options.Animation = new ChartAnimation { Loop = true }</code>.</li>
                <li>Set <code>Duration</code>, in milliseconds, and <code>Easing</code> to control the speed and feel of each pass.</li>
                <li>Set <code>Loop = false</code> and call <code>UpdateAsync</code> to stop it. The demo calls <code>StopAsync</code> and <code>ResetAsync</code> first, so the chart replays from its starting state with the new setting.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>
    </Block>
    <Demo Type="typeof(LineChart_Demo_56_Animation_Loop)" Tabs="true" />
</SampleSection>

<SampleSection Slug="progress-bar">
    <Block>
        <code>Animation.OnProgress</code> and <code>OnComplete</code> call registered JavaScript functions while a chart animates. The demo uses them to move a <code>&lt;progress&gt;</code> element.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>How to use:</strong>
        <div class="content">
            <ol>
                <li>Add a <code>&lt;progress&gt;</code> element with an <code>id</code> to your page.</li>
                <li>Register the two functions shown below in your own script file. Chart.js passes each one the animation, with <code>currentStep</code> and <code>numSteps</code>.</li>
                <li>Set <code>Options.Animation</code> to a <code>ChartAnimation</code> with <code>OnProgress = new ChartFunction("demo.showAnimationProgress")</code> and <code>OnComplete = new ChartFunction("demo.completeAnimationProgress")</code>.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>
    </Block>
    <CodeChunk Class="mb-3" LanguageCode="LanguageCode.JavaScript" FilePath="~\Pages\Demos\LineChart\LineChart_Demo_57_Progress_Bar_Snippet.js" ProjectRootClassType="DemoAppConstants.ProjectRootClassType" />
    <Demo Type="typeof(LineChart_Demo_57_Progress_Bar)" Tabs="true" />
</SampleSection>
</CascadingValue>

